{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"FIXED — branch `fix/8455-failed-call-usage` (worktree `wt-8455`), not yet pushed; the orchestrator\ncherry-picks it onto the Gerrit stack.\n\n## Mechanism, re-confirmed at runtime (not just re-read)\n\nThe ticket's mechanism held exactly as described. Confirmed by executing it rather than by reading:\na `FunctionModel` whose function raises, driven through the real `PydanticAIRunner.run()` with\n`REBAR_USAGE_LOG` pointed at a temp file, raised `LLMUnavailableError` (message preserved, original\n`RuntimeError` as `__cause__` — so `interpret_failure` did its job) and left the log file\n**non-existent**. That is the live Bedrock 400 reproduced offline in one test.\n\n## Fix — inside `runner.py`'s own `except`, as scoped\n\n`structured_run.py` was off-limits (another agent owns it) and holds `interpret_failure`, so the fix\nnever touches the spine. Two files:\n\n**`src/rebar/llm/runner.py`** (+6, now 699 LOC) — six lines in `run()`'s own `except` block,\nimmediately BEFORE `interpret_failure(...)`: `usage_log.record_failure(run_messages, _call_label,\nran_model, req_limit, eff_max_iter)`, plus a WHY comment naming this bug. Nothing else moved; the\nsuccess-path `record()` at the end of `run()` is untouched.\n\n**`src/rebar/llm/usage_log.py`** (+77, now 468 LOC) —\n* `OUTCOME_OK = \"ok\"` / `OUTCOME_FAILED = \"failed\"`, written under an `\"outcome\"` key on **every**\n  row. Explicit on BOTH kinds, per the AC: absence is already this writer's truthful encoding of\n  \"not applicable\" (`model`/`step`/`model_class` are omitted, never nulled), so overloading absence\n  to ALSO mean \"this call failed\" would have made the two unreadable.\n* `record()` gains keyword-only `outcome: str = OUTCOME_OK`, so all pre-existing callers are\n  behaviourally unchanged. The `not path or not usage` no-op guard is untouched.\n* New `record_failure(...)`: resolves `step`/`model_class` off the `step_identity` ContextVar\n  (available at failure time exactly as the ticket's live-confirmation comment noted), derives\n  counters from `failure_usage(messages, request_limit=…, tool_calls_limit=max(8, eff_max_iter))` —\n  mirroring `interpret_failure`'s own `max(8, eff_max_iter)` so the two diagnostics of one failure\n  agree — and calls `record(..., outcome=OUTCOME_FAILED)`.\n\n`summarize()` is deliberately **unchanged**: the failure row folds into the totals, which is the\npoint — the spend total now includes failed calls.\n\n### Never breaking the call path\n\n`record_failure`'s entire body sits in one `try/except Exception` that logs and swallows, not just\nthe write (which `record()` already guards). This is load-bearing, not defensive habit: its caller\nis an `except` block whose one job is to re-raise the provider's exception, so anything escaping\ntelemetry would REPLACE the real diagnosis. Two tests pin it — the provider error's type, message\nand `__cause__` survive, and an unwritable log path degrades telemetry only.\n\n### No double-counting\n\nThree ways, all tested:\n1. **One row per `run()` call, not per attempt.** `run_messages` accumulates every internal\n   pydantic-ai request/retry attempt, and `record_failure` folds them into a single row. A\n   step-budget runaway making N model requests writes ONE row with `requests == N`, not N rows —\n   otherwise a runaway's spend would be multiplied by its own step count.\n2. **Success and failure paths are mutually exclusive** within one `run()`: the tail `record()` is\n   reached only when nothing escaped, and `record_failure` only from the `except`.\n3. **A retried call**: the failed attempt's row is `outcome=\"failed\"` and the retry's is\n   `outcome=\"ok\"`, so exactly one row is attributable to delivered work. Burned budget is separable\n   from delivered work by reading one explicit field.\n\n## Test — RED first, then held out from the implementer\n\nNew file `tests/unit/test_usage_log_failed_calls.py` (10 tests). Per the AC every test drives the\nreal runner with a model that RAISES and then READS the file; nothing asserts on `usage_log.py`\nsource and nothing exercises `failure_usage()` in isolation. Offline throughout (`FunctionModel` +\n`ALLOW_MODEL_REQUESTS = False`), so no billable call.\n\n* **RED confirmed for the right reason:** 7 failed / 3 passed before any fix. The 7 all failed on\n  the empty log while the run correctly raised `LLMError`; the 3 that passed are the guard tests\n  (no-op-when-unset, error-not-converted, unwritable-sink) which must be green in both states.\n* **Held out:** the file was moved OUT of the worktree before the implementation subagent ran; it\n  got only a contract card (postconditions + invariants), never the fixture, assertions or path.\n  All 10 passed on first re-application — GREEN against an oracle the implementer never saw.\n* **Teeth (two defect-seeded mutations):** deleting the `record_failure` call returns exactly the\n  original 7 RED; flipping the failure row's marker to `OUTCOME_OK` returns 2 RED — the\n  distinguishability test AND the double-counting test, so the marker is genuinely load-bearing and\n  not decoration.\n\n## Gates\n\n`make lint` PASS · `make typecheck` PASS (mypy, 407 files) · targeted suites 79 passed\n(`test_usage_log`, `test_usage_log_attribution`, `test_usage_log_failed_calls`,\n`test_structured_run_seam`, `test_runner_pydantic_budget`, `test_interpret_failure_seam`,\n`workflow/test_code_review_usage_attribution`) · full `tests/unit` green · module-size gate clear\n(runner 699 / usage_log 468, cap 800 — and runner stays under the 700-LOC headroom target\n`test_structured_run_seam` enforces, which is why the helper lives in `usage_log.py`).\n\n## Sibling sweep\n\nNone. `usage_log.record` has exactly ONE call site in `src/` (the runner) and it is the one fixed;\nthis was a single-site defect, not a recurring pattern.\n\n## Note for whoever reads the log next\n\nRows written before this change have no `\"outcome\"` key at all. Anything separating delivered work\nfrom burned budget should read `row.get(\"outcome\", \"ok\")` — a pre-8455 row can only be a successful\ncall, since a failed one wrote nothing."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785629876793389001,"uuid":"2f4c5df3-f331-47e8-9bfb-447fb2c2ef3e"}